From f90bdda2ba8e444eb3eb1b39b6e0170e63fbdde9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 17:36:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Post-processings: each one copies to the host the arrays it
 reads

Before the post-processings, the non linear evolution problems made valid
on the host all the arrays of all the materials: gradients, thermodynamic
forces, internal state variables and energies of both states, and the
tangent operator blocks. After a computation on a GPU, this copied all of
them to the host at each time step, even when the post-processings read
nothing there: the mean values of the thermodynamic forces computed by
kernels (MFEM_MGIS_POST_PROCESSINGS_COMPUTATION=Kernels) read the
thermodynamic forces on the device.

This synchronization is removed, before the post-processings and before the
initial post-processings: the arrays follow the rule of `MaterialArrays`,
each code accessing them on the host making valid there what it reads,
when it reads it. The post-processings of mfem-mgis already do it, through
the accessors of the materials (`getThermodynamicForce`,
`getInternalStateVariable`...), `computeMeanThermodynamicForcesValues`,
`computeResultantForceOnBoundary`, the energies, and the export of the
results at the integration points (parameter `Results`), which reads them
at each export. A post-processing given as a function does the same, as
the ones of mm-opera-hpc and of mfem-mgis-examples do; the documentation
of `addPostProcessing`, of `executePostProcessings`, of the functions
given to the export at the integration points and of `StateManager` says
it. On the host, without device, the synchronizations do nothing: the
results are the same.

For a polycrystal of 600 grains on one node (4 MI300A, 4.9 M unknowns per
GPU, 20 time steps, mean stresses by kernels), the post-processings take
0.92 s against 2.44 s, 1.55 s of which copied the arrays.

MaterialArraysTest: after all the arrays are written on the device, the
post-processings of the test (mean values of the thermodynamic forces,
resultant force) copy one array to the host, the thermodynamic forces at
the end of the time step; the internal state variables stay on the device.
